271
00:24:36,901 --> 00:24:40,900
This attractive wonder is a so-called permanent magnet.

272
00:24:40,901 --> 00:24:46,280
permanent magnet, an iron-bearing metal that retains its magnetism after the removal of

273
00:24:46,281 --> 00:24:49,041
the magnetic force.

274
00:24:49,042 --> 00:24:59,940
That force can either be another permanent magnet or a jolt of electricity.

275
00:24:59,941 --> 00:25:06,740
Another form of magnet, an electromagnet, is magnetic only when energized by electric current.

276
00:25:06,741 --> 00:25:10,620
Combine electricity with any piece of iron, and you've got an electromagnet.

277
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:16,200
This is a piece of ordinary iron.

278
00:25:16,380 --> 00:25:17,380
It's not magnetic.

279
00:25:17,860 --> 00:25:20,520
It won't pick up the other bolts.

280
00:25:21,080 --> 00:25:25,820
To make it magnetic, I'm going to wrap this wire around this bar.

281
00:25:27,200 --> 00:25:34,860
Now, when I run electric current through this coil of wire, it will turn into a magnet, an electromagnet.

282
00:25:36,740 --> 00:25:39,700
And now it picks up the iron.

283
00:25:41,500 --> 00:25:44,900
But if I cut the power, the magnetism goes away.

284
00:25:46,200 --> 00:25:50,300
This property is the key to movement in electric motors that power our industry.

285
00:25:54,700 --> 00:26:02,440
An electromagnet called the armature revolves between the north and south poles of a stationary permanent magnet called the stator.

286
00:26:02,441 --> 00:26:09,440
The armature rotates until its north pole is opposite the south pole of the stator.

287
00:26:09,441 --> 00:26:14,440
The direction of the armature's current is then reversed.

288
00:26:14,441 --> 00:26:19,440
The two south poles repel each other, pushing the armature forward.

289
00:26:19,441 --> 00:26:26,440
The current reverses every half turn, keeping the armature in motion.

